 that he can still see  the good in humans even when faced with  overwhelming evidence to the contrary at  its core the idea of trying to make the  world a better place in your small  corner of the solar system and following  your conscience with no expectation of a  reward in hopes that others might do the  same comes out again and again and even  though the politics are complicated and  a purely happy ending feels like it  would be pretty fragile having drummer  end up as the trade union president is a  satisfying conclusion the show switched  some things up and holden accepting the  job just to resign and i like the added  dimension of them outplaying avasarala  as the way to make that happen marco  relearned the lesson that the inners  will never see belters as equals the  hard way and becoming duarte's useful  idiot and i always thought of philip's  ending as being the product of being  born into an impossible situation he's  given a chance to maybe make up for his  crimes but he has to do it himself if  you think about it he can't really even  try to reunite with his mother  considering the circumstances she has to  mourn his death in a situation where she  chose the family she found and there's a  nice line from just before what i read  at the beginning that sums this up i  will carry this guilt and sadness for  the rest of my life was just as true as  i lost my son years ago her comfort was  that she was still alive that jim was  and amos and alex bobby and clarissa  there's the idea that both of these  things can exist together without  necessarily canceling each other out and  i think that ties into what we heard at  the beginning of all the x-ray features  this season in the doctrine of one ship  there's just one hole with countless  parts working together like the cells of  a single organism there are factions and  tribes in different political interests  inequalities built into the system and  exploitation that creates conditions  where people like marc